    "Which specific method of configuration is required for the constraints (e.g., Fluent API or DataAnnotations)?": {
        "answer": "Based on the provided context, it is specified that DataAnnotations should be used for configuring the constraints. This is explicitly mentioned for the Department, Instructor, and implicitly for the Student model. \n\nFor example, in the Department model, it is stated \"Make Name => DeptName using DataAnnotations\", indicating that DataAnnotations should be used to configure the DeptName property. Similarly, in the Instructor model, it is stated \"Make Name => Ins_Name using DataAnnotations\" and \"Make Id => Ins_Id using DataAnnotations\", indicating that DataAnnotations should be used to configure the Ins_Name and Ins_Id properties.\n\nTherefore, the specific method of configuration required for the constraints is DataAnnotations. This means that attributes such as [Required], [StringLength], [MinLength], and [MaxLength] will be used to decorate the properties in the models to enforce the specified constraints.\n\nFor instance, the Student model might be configured as follows:\n```csharp\npublic class Student\n{\n    [StringLength(20, MinimumLength = 3)]\n    public string StdName { get; set; }\n    // Other properties...\n}\n```\nSimilarly, the Department and Instructor models will be configured using DataAnnotations to enforce their respective constraints.",
